{
  "dom_id": "question-363836",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>\n        <table cellpadding=\"0\">\n          <tbody>\n            <tr>\n              <td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\" height=\"30px\" align=\"right\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"90px\" align=\"center\">\n                $1$\n              </td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n            </tr>\n            <tr>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n            </tr>\n            <tr>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n            </tr>\n          </tbody>\n        </table>\n      </td>\n      <td>\n        <table cellpadding=\"0\">\n          <tbody>\n            <tr>\n              <td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\" height=\"30px\" align=\"right\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"90px\" align=\"center\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n            </tr>\n            <tr>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td align=\"center\">\n                $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}$\n              </td>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n            </tr>\n            <tr>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n              <td align=\"center\"></td>\n              <td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n            </tr>\n          </tbody>\n        </table>\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
